6f:T508,## Problem

A working photographer in Pune, with more than ten years behind the camera, needed a portfolio that matched the quality of the work, and a way for clients to book studio time without a back-and-forth of messages.

## Approach

A high-end portfolio organised by service, with a full showcase for each, and a studio rental site alongside it. Images lead; the interface stays out of their way. It is built with plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript, with GSAP for scroll-driven motion and Swiper for the galleries.

## What's on the site

- **A page per service.** Weddings, pre-wedding, fashion and portfolios, corporate and commercial, interiors and architecture, and lifestyle, each with its own gallery.
- **Portfolio catalogue.** Selected images by category, opening in a full-screen viewer.
- **Studio 41.** A separate section for the photographer's rental studio in Pune: the space and its specifications, switchable sets, lighting and grip, talent and styling support, and a booking call to action.
- **Contact.** An enquiry page and a floating WhatsApp button on every page, because that is how most clients get in touch.
- **Search.** A sitemap, robots file and page-level metadata aimed at local searches for a photographer in Pune.

## Outcome

Live at ajinkyajadhav.co.in.
70:Td30,## Problem

In most hotels an emergency starts with a walkie-talkie. Someone hits a panic button, someone else radios a room number, and the right people with the right skills may or may not be listening. The minutes lost in that hand-off are the ones that matter.

## Approach

Treat the alert as the start of a plan, not just a notification. When an alert arrives, Gemini reads the incident and writes a two-to-three sentence situation brief along with an action checklist tailored to the emergency type. Tasks are then assigned to staff who are available nearby, so every action has an owner before anyone reaches the scene. The target is under three seconds from button press to a briefed team.

If nobody acknowledges within 90 seconds, Gemini writes an escalation message and the duty manager is alerted automatically. Each resolved incident feeds an analytics pass in Vertex AI that surfaces hotspots and peak-risk times from the last 30 days.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  P[Panic panel] --> A[FastAPI]
  W[Guest web trigger] --> A
  A --> F[(Firestore)]
  A --> G[Gemini]
  G --> A
  A --> T[Task allocation]
  T --> N[Push notifications]
  A <--> D[Live dashboard]
  F --> V[Vertex AI insights]
```

## Incident lifecycle

1. **Alert triggered.** A structured payload with type, room, device and timestamp goes to the backend.
2. **Incident logged.** The alert is validated and stored in Firestore, so there is an auditable record from the first second.
3. **Gemini brief.** A situation summary and a checklist of recommended actions, written for this incident rather than pulled from a template.
4. **Task allocation.** Checklist items go to staff who are on duty and nearby.
5. **Staff notified.** The dashboard updates over Socket.IO, so every open screen shows the same incident state.
6. **Acknowledge and resolve.** Responders claim their task and move it from responding to resolved. Each change is broadcast live.
7. **Escalation.** No acknowledgement in 90 seconds sends a context-aware message to the duty manager.
8. **Risk insights.** Resolved incidents feed a 30-day pattern analysis.

## Three ways to raise an alert

- **A hardware panic device.** A handheld panel with one button each for medical, fire, security and distress, a press-and-hold microphone for a voice description, and an LED and buzzer to confirm the alert went out. The design uses BLE and LoRaWAN so it keeps working without Wi-Fi. Firmware is in the repo.
- **A guest web trigger.** A phone screen with the same four choices, a required room number, voice activation and a swipe to confirm, so an alert can't be sent by accident.
- **The API.** `POST /api/alert` takes the same payload, which is how the hardware and the web trigger both report in.

## The operations dashboard

- **Stats bar** for total, active, responding and resolved incidents, plus today's count.
- **Active critical alerts** as colour-coded cards, each with its Gemini brief and checklist.
- **Incident breakdown** by type, and a list of responders on duty with their floor and skills.
- **Incident log** with timestamps, responder names and resolution notes.
- **Risk insights** tab for the 30-day analysis.

## Outcome

Selected as a **Top 100** project in the Google Solution Challenge 2026. Built as a team; this case study covers the coordination pipeline, the AI brief and the dashboard.
71:T8e0,## Problem

Around 190 million people in India have no formal credit history, so lenders can't score them and they can't borrow. The gap isn't creditworthiness. It's data.

## Approach

CredZen builds an alternative credit signal from financial behaviour the person agrees to share: bank activity through the Account Aggregator framework, GST filings and telecom payment history. It returns a score between 300 and 850 that explains itself, in about thirty seconds, so the person and the lender can both see why the number is what it is.

## How a score is produced

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  U[Mobile number + OTP] --> C[Consent: bank, GST, telecom]
  C --> D[Fetch consented data]
  D --> E[Feature engineering]
  E --> M[Model inference]
  M --> S[SHAP explanation]
  S --> R[Applicant report]
  S --> L[Lender dashboard]
```

1. **Sign in and consent.** The applicant picks which data sources to share. Any source they decline is filled with neutral defaults rather than counted against them.
2. **Feature engineering.** Raw monthly records become three derived signals: financial resilience, income stability and spending discipline.
3. **Scoring.** A trained tree-based model predicts the probability of creditworthiness, which maps to the 300 to 850 range and a risk band.
4. **Explanation.** SHAP values show which factors pushed the score up or down, with the strongest positives and negatives called out.
5. **Roadmap.** The report ends with specific steps to improve, each with an estimated gain and timeframe.

## What the applicant and the lender see

- **Applicant report.** The score dial, risk band, category scores for payments, income stability and discipline, a SHAP impact chart and the improvement roadmap. It can be downloaded or shared with a lender by link.
- **Lender dashboard.** The same report plus a lender-readiness rating and the applicant's percentile among peers.

## Honest limits

This is a working prototype. The data connectors are simulated, the OTP step is a demo, and sessions live in memory for thirty minutes. The scoring pipeline, the model and the explanations are real; the next step is replacing the mock fetcher with live consented connectors.

## Outcome

A working demo is live, and the model training code is open source.
72:T97a,## Problem

Getting from "something looks wrong" to the right doctor takes too many separate steps: a scan in one place, a report in another, a referral on paper, and health records the patient never really holds.

## Approach

MediQ puts the whole path in one platform, with separate experiences for patients and for doctors. A patient can ask a health question, upload a skin photo or a chest X-ray for AI screening, get a report, find a hospital and book an appointment. A doctor reviews the same scan and report, and can refer the patient on to a specialist.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  S[Scan upload] --> A[AI screening]
  A --> R[Report]
  R --> D[Doctor review]
  D --> F[Specialist referral]
  D --> B[Appointment]
```

The screening is decision support, not a diagnosis. Every result is meant to be reviewed by a doctor.

## For patients

- **AI health assistant.** Symptom questions and medication information, answered through retrieval-augmented generation over a Pinecone vector index.
- **Image screening.** Chest X-rays are screened for pneumonia and other chest conditions, and skin photos for dermatological conditions. Both models run on ONNX Runtime, and a Grad-CAM heatmap shows which part of the image the model looked at.
- **Reports and history.** A timeline of scans and results, with a downloadable PDF report.
- **Doctors and appointments.** Find a specialist, read reviews and book.
- **Hospital finder.** Nearby hospitals and clinics based on location.
- **A health ID** on every profile, plus consent and privacy controls over who can see the record.
- **Three languages.** English, Hindi and Marathi.

## For doctors and specialists

- **Dashboard** for availability, appointments and schedule.
- **Patient records** with history, reports and earlier scans.
- **Referrals.** Search for a specialist, set a priority, add clinical notes and track the status. Each referral keeps an audit log of every change.
- **Specialist inbox.** Incoming referrals with the scan, the heatmap, the report and the referring doctor's remarks, ready to accept or decline.

## Under the hood

A React and TypeScript front end built with Vite, Tailwind and shadcn/ui talks to a FastAPI backend. Data lives in MongoDB through the async Motor driver, authentication is JWT with separate roles for patients, doctors and specialists, passwords are hashed with bcrypt, and reports are generated server-side with ReportLab.
